WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.536
Hi, I'm Juniper!

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:06.689
Carveout turns a trained Gaussian-splat scene into labelled 3D objects.

00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:12.780
No retraining, no original photos.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.112
Carveout renders its own views, segments them, and lifts every mask onto

00:00:17.112 --> 00:00:18.917
the Gaussians that drew it.

00:00:21.008 --> 00:00:22.861
Start from a trained splat.

00:00:22.861 --> 00:00:27.307
Carveout places synthetic cameras inside a volume you approve, and renders views.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:31.836
SAM three runs a confirmed list of noun phrases over every view,

00:00:31.836 --> 00:00:34.918
logging every raw score, so thresholds come from data.

00:00:35.338 --> 00:00:37.062
Then the hard part, the lift.

00:00:37.062 --> 00:00:40.797
Each 2D mask is attributed back to the Gaussians that rendered into it.

00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:45.629
Out come objects with position, extents, a box and a confidence.

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:49.239
An optional vision model confirms, relabels or rejects each.

00:00:51.008 --> 00:00:54.521
Here's the workbench after a run: every object labelled and scored,

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:55.799
on the live splat.

00:00:56.218 --> 00:00:58.695
Salon chairs, barber chairs, each with its score.

00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:02.887
Say a Gaussian is seen in twenty views.

00:01:02.887 --> 00:01:06.593
Three say salon chair, one says barber chair, sixteen say nothing.

00:01:06.593 --> 00:01:07.604
One rule decides.

00:01:08.024 --> 00:01:11.789
Set every colour to one, render, and multiply by the mask.

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:14.869
The gradient is each Gaussian's share of masked pixels.

00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:16.237
The biggest class wins.

00:01:16.657 --> 00:01:19.498
T is its mass in every view that saw it.

00:01:19.498 --> 00:01:22.624
A view that detected nothing is a vote against every label.

00:01:23.044 --> 00:01:25.076
Gamma sets the bar, tuned per class.

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:31.154
The limit is memory: about twelve bytes per Gaussian per class.

00:01:31.154 --> 00:01:35.541
On a twenty-four gigabyte card, thirty million Gaussians get about sixty classes.

00:01:37.017 --> 00:01:40.172
Each view votes with the mass the Gaussian shows in it: large

00:01:40.172 --> 00:01:42.275
and frontal beats a sliver at the edge.

00:01:45.942 --> 00:01:49.691
To recap: render, detect, then lift each mask by weighted vote.

00:01:50.542 --> 00:01:52.678
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:53.288
I'm Juniper.

00:01:53.288 --> 00:01:53.593
Bye!
